file_merger: a timestamp keeps the fraction its source had (checkpoint 1)

The user's wording, "keeping fractional seconds" (the draft said "keeping any fractional
seconds"), no judge run. 2026-09-20.

Sentence: "`timestamp` normalized to UTC with `Z` (e.g., `2024-07-01T12:00:00Z`); if source
lacked zone, treat as UTC", under Deterministic Dialect Details. The only other words on the
format are "ISO-8601 format" (line 86), and ISO 8601 allows a fraction without asking for one.
Tests spec_correct/type_parsing_edgecases (checkpoint 1) and hidden/nested_timestamp_microseconds
(checkpoint 4) expect `.123456Z`, `.654321Z` and `.000001Z` kept; the reference appends the
microseconds, trailing zeros trimmed, when they are not zero. No fixture has a trailing zero,
so trimmed against six digits is untested and this sentence takes no side.

min13-ABDJKMNT on v6, repeat (20260920T0700) chose whole seconds: registry T8, Risk 35,
"'Deterministic Dialect Details' asks for one fixed output shape, and the worked example shows
exactly seconds precision". Its first run (20260920T0545) asked the same question, T10, Risk 25,
and kept the fraction: "Dropping data the source carried seems worse than keeping it." History:
type_parsing_edgecases 22 pass of 24 runs, nested_timestamp_microseconds 19 of 21 reached; the
two failures are this run and sonnet-4.6 just-solve. A rare coin, not a shared reading. The
checkpoint-4 miss comes from the checkpoint-1 formatter, so checkpoint 4 needs no copy.

Over-correction to avoid: wording that reads as "always print six digits", which would turn
`12:00:00Z` into `12:00:00.000000Z` and fail every whole-second fixture.

